1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:06,052
Tilt the board and read how gravity moves between X, Y and Z. This is a longer

2
00:00:06,052 --> 00:00:09,540
lesson. We'll build a toolbox first, then use it.

3
00:00:10,540 --> 00:00:17,979
Open 04 i2c accelerometer reference. Your project needs the i2c driver. Paste both

4
00:00:17,979 --> 00:00:19,340
files and save.

5
00:00:20,340 --> 00:00:24,980
Lines 69 to 72: board, pins, clock and text.

6
00:00:25,480 --> 00:00:29,000
Line 73 is the I2C driver.

7
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:36,140
Lines 74 and 75 let us control two pins by hand, which we'll need soon.

8
00:00:37,140 --> 00:00:41,940
Switch on lines 111 to 128. These are names for important numbers.

9
00:00:42,440 --> 00:00:51,827
Line 111: we use the I2C block number zero. Line 112: speed, 100,000 bits per

10
00:00:51,827 --> 00:00:52,680
second.

11
00:00:53,180 --> 00:00:59,808
Lines 114 to 118 name five mailboxes inside the sensor. Zero x means the number is

12
00:00:59,808 --> 00:01:01,020
written in hex.

13
00:01:01,520 --> 00:01:05,280
Line 116 is the who am I mailbox.

14
00:01:05,780 --> 00:01:12,420
Lines 120 and 121 are the two answers we accept, one for each possible sensor chip.

15
00:01:12,920 --> 00:01:23,433
Lines 123 and 124 are the clock pin, 24, and data pin, 25. Line 125 is a short

16
00:01:23,433 --> 00:01:25,320
waiting count.

17
00:01:25,820 --> 00:01:32,780
Line 127 lists four possible addresses. The word const means the list never changes.

18
00:01:33,280 --> 00:01:38,720
Line 128 remembers which address we're using, starting at hex 1 D.

19
00:01:39,720 --> 00:01:45,796
Switch on lines 164 to 201. Sometimes the sensor is left holding the data wire low.

20
00:01:45,796 --> 00:01:47,480
This function frees it.

21
00:01:47,980 --> 00:01:53,740
Lines 166 to 168 make two settings forms and a counter.

22
00:01:54,240 --> 00:01:56,880
Line 170 wakes port E.

23
00:01:57,380 --> 00:02:05,860
Lines 171 to 174 make both pins plain pins, with a gentle pull up.

24
00:02:06,360 --> 00:02:13,480
Lines 176 to 179 make both pins outputs that start high.

25
00:02:13,980 --> 00:02:19,340
Line 181 pulls the data wire low. Line 182 waits a moment.

26
00:02:20,340 --> 00:02:23,060
Line 184 repeats nine times.

27
00:02:23,560 --> 00:02:32,760
Line 186 drops the clock, line 187 releases the data, and line 188 raises the clock.

28
00:02:33,260 --> 00:02:37,420
Each trip is one pulse, letting the sensor finish its byte.

29
00:02:37,920 --> 00:02:45,680
Lines 191 to 194 send a stop signal by hand: the data rises while the clock is high.

30
00:02:46,180 --> 00:02:53,780
Lines 197 to 200 hand both pins to the real I2C hardware, using setting number five.

31
00:02:54,780 --> 00:02:59,900
Switch on lines 240 to 269. Two helpers do all the talking.

32
00:03:00,400 --> 00:03:06,160
Line 240: write one value into one mailbox. It hands back a status.

33
00:03:06,660 --> 00:03:15,460
Line 242 makes an empty delivery form. Line 243 puts our value into a one item list.

34
00:03:15,960 --> 00:03:21,400
Line 245: which address. Line 246: the direction, write.

35
00:03:21,900 --> 00:03:28,780
Lines 247 and 248: which mailbox, and that the mailbox number is one byte.

36
00:03:29,280 --> 00:03:35,920
Lines 249 to 251: the data, its size, and normal transfer.

37
00:03:36,420 --> 00:03:42,180
Line 253 sends the form and waits until finished. That's what blocking means.

38
00:03:43,180 --> 00:03:46,780
Line 256 reads one or more mailboxes into a buffer.

39
00:03:47,280 --> 00:03:58,328
Line 261 sets the direction to read. Lines 264 and 265 say where to put the bytes,

40
00:03:58,328 --> 00:04:00,080
and how many.

41
00:04:00,580 --> 00:04:03,380
Which single idea separates these two helpers?

42
00:04:03,880 --> 00:04:08,880
Your turn. Pause the video.

43
00:04:08,880 --> 00:04:10,400
The direction of the data.

44
00:04:11,400 --> 00:04:14,680
Switch on lines 310 to 361.

45
00:04:15,180 --> 00:04:20,140
Line 310 returns true or false: did we find the sensor?

46
00:04:20,640 --> 00:04:23,440
Line 315 visits each address in the list.

47
00:04:23,940 --> 00:04:32,260
Line 317 chooses the current address. Line 319 asks the who am I mailbox.

48
00:04:32,760 --> 00:04:42,305
If something answers, line 321 prints what it said. Lines 323 to 325 return true if

49
00:04:42,305 --> 00:04:45,640
the answer is a chip we know.

50
00:04:46,140 --> 00:04:52,620
If nothing answers, line 330 reports no ACK, meaning no acknowledgment.

51
00:04:53,120 --> 00:04:57,280
Line 334 returns false when every address fails.

52
00:04:58,280 --> 00:05:04,120
Lines 341 and 342 free the wires, then hand the pins to I2C.

53
00:05:04,620 --> 00:05:10,940
Lines 344 to 346 set up the I2C hardware at our speed.

54
00:05:11,440 --> 00:05:19,600
Line 350: if nothing is found, line 352 explains, and line 353 stops here forever.

55
00:05:20,100 --> 00:05:24,500
Line 358 puts the sensor to sleep, so settings can change.

56
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:30,520
Line 359 chooses the range: plus or minus four g.

57
00:05:31,020 --> 00:05:35,900
Line 360 sets 200 readings per second and wakes it.

58
00:05:36,900 --> 00:05:39,940
Switch on lines 396 to 410.

59
00:05:40,440 --> 00:05:46,865
Line 396 hands back three answers, using pointers: the stars mean the function

60
00:05:46,865 --> 00:05:48,760
writes into your boxes.

61
00:05:49,260 --> 00:05:56,140
Lines 398 and 399 make space for six raw bytes and three numbers.

62
00:05:56,640 --> 00:06:00,240
Line 401 reads all six in one trip.

63
00:06:00,740 --> 00:06:10,049
Line 403 joins two bytes for X, then shifts right by two to drop empty bits. Lines

64
00:06:10,049 --> 00:06:12,660
404 and 405 do Y and Z.

65
00:06:13,160 --> 00:06:20,920
Lines 407 to 409 convert to milli g: times 1000, divided by 2048 counts per g.

66
00:06:21,920 --> 00:06:29,760
Now inside main. Switch on lines 438 to 442. They wake the board and print a title.

67
00:06:30,260 --> 00:06:34,340
Switch on line 470. One call runs the whole bring up.

68
00:06:34,840 --> 00:06:39,974
Build and load. Your board may answer at a different address. That's fine. Write

69
00:06:39,974 --> 00:06:40,680
yours down.

70
00:06:41,680 --> 00:06:44,640
Switch on lines 500 to 513.

71
00:06:45,140 --> 00:06:53,203
Line 500 loops forever. Lines 502 and 503 make boxes for the answers and a wait

72
00:06:53,203 --> 00:06:54,020
counter.

73
00:06:54,520 --> 00:07:00,440
Line 505 reads the tilt. The and signs say where to put each answer.

74
00:07:00,940 --> 00:07:02,780
Line 507 prints them.

75
00:07:03,280 --> 00:07:08,960
Lines 509 to 512 count to a million, so you can read the text.

76
00:07:09,460 --> 00:07:13,620
With the board flat, which value should be near 1000?

77
00:07:14,120 --> 00:07:19,120
Your turn. Pause the video.

78
00:07:19,120 --> 00:07:19,760
Z.

79
00:07:20,760 --> 00:07:25,160
On line 509, use a smaller count to print faster.

80
00:07:25,660 --> 00:07:30,409
Then change the range to two g, changing both places together. If you change only

81
00:07:30,409 --> 00:07:33,340
one, your readings will be off by a factor of two.

82
00:07:33,840 --> 00:07:38,406
Excellent. You've read an I2C sensor from the wires all the way to meaningful

83
00:07:38,406 --> 00:07:38,880
numbers.

